<article class="env-lemma" id="06RR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06RR">Lemma <span data-tag="06RR">10.153.2</span>.</a> Let $(R, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a local ring. Let $f \in R[T]$. Let $a, b \in R$ such that $f(a) = f(b) = 0$, $a = b \bmod \mathfrak m$, and $f'(a) \not\in \mathfrak m$. Then $a = b$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Write $f(x + y) - f(x) = f'(x)y + g(x, y) y^2$ in $R[x, y]$ (this is possible as one sees by expanding $f(x + y)$; details omitted). Then we see that $0 = f(b) - f(a) = f(a + (b - a)) - f(a) = f'(a)(b - a) + c (b - a)^2$ for some $c \in R$. By assumption $f'(a)$ is a unit in $R$. Hence $(b - a)(1 + f'(a)^{-1}c(b - a)) = 0$. By assumption $b - a \in \mathfrak m$, hence $1 + f'(a)^{-1}c(b - a)$ is a unit in $R$. Hence $b - a = 0$ in $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>